# The brute-force remark and the "maybe two pointers" plan in the first
# utterance aren't steps yet, so they change nothing. Keeping them as a
# note is equally faithful.
step 1
or
note root general: trying every pair is naive; maybe two pointers

step 2
add root:
    left = 0
    right = len(height) - 1
    best = 0
expect gaps WARN001 WARN001 WARN001

step 3
add root: while left < right:
    area = (right - left) * min(height[left], height[right])
expect gaps WARN001 WARN001

step 4
add n15: best = max(best, area)
expect gaps none

step 5
add n15:
    if height[left] < height[right]:
        left += 1
    else:
        right -= 1
expect gaps none
or
add n15:
    if height[left] < height[right]:
        left = left + 1
    else:
        right = right - 1
expect gaps none

# The reason for the rule is a comment on it, not a step.
step 6
note n40 general: the shorter line limits the area, so moving the taller one can't help
expect gaps none
or
expect gaps none

step 7
add root: return best
expect gaps none

step 8
note root complexity: O(n), since every step moves one of the pointers
expect gaps none
